<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Part one: MATH 13 (about 11 minutes) -->
<section xml:id="sec-math13">
  <title>MATH 13: Calculus and Analytic Geometry III</title>

  <!-- Notes: Same mathematics as a traditional third-quarter text, written as a web book
       from the start; the PDF is a by-product. Numbers straight from the repository.
       Supported by the Teaching and Technology Innovation Grant with Norman Paris. -->
  <slide>
    <title>A web book first, a printed text second</title>
    <p>Hyperbolic functions · series and Taylor approximation · vectors, lines, planes, and quadric surfaces · partial derivatives, gradients, extreme values, and Lagrange multipliers.</p>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>169</cell><cell>37</cell><cell>58</cell><cell>205</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>accessible diagrams</cell><cell>live figures</cell><cell>short animations</cell><cell>exercises</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>PreFigure, every part described</cell><cell>Plotly, Three.js, GeoGebra, Desmos</cell><cell>Manim videos of constructions</cell><cell>solutions after the due date</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>Counted from the book's source repository, October 2026.</p>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: The applications are where the mathematics is worked out: the catenary with
       hyperbolic functions, each quadric with a real object, conics with whispering
       galleries, LORAN, and comet Borisov. -->
  <slide>
    <title>The mathematics is worked out where it lives</title>
    <sidebyside widths="23% 23% 23% 23%" valign="top">
      <figure>
        <caption>Catenary: the Gateway Arch</caption>
        <image source="images/photo-arch.jpg"><shortdescription>The Gateway Arch in St. Louis against a clear sky.</shortdescription></image>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <caption>Hyperboloid of one sheet: cooling towers</caption>
        <image source="images/photo-tower.jpg"><shortdescription>Two cooling towers with hyperboloid profiles.</shortdescription></image>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <caption>Paraboloid: the Very Large Array</caption>
        <image source="images/photo-vla.jpg"><shortdescription>Radio dishes of the Very Large Array.</shortdescription></image>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <caption>Hyperbolic paraboloid: the Saddledome</caption>
        <image source="images/photo-saddledome.jpg"><shortdescription>The saddle-shaped roof of the Saddledome in Calgary.</shortdescription></image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
    <p>Also in the book: whispering galleries, LORAN navigation, an interstellar comet's orbit, the torque on a wrench, a dipole's field, isobars around a low, and the small-angle pendulum.</p>
  </slide>

  <!-- ===== How it's coded: Photos and alt text (3 steps) ===== -->
  <slide>
    <title>A figure around the image</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Photos and alt text · step 1 of 3.</alert> A <c>&lt;figure&gt;</c> gives the photo a number and a caption. <c>&lt;image source&gt;</c> points at the file and sets its width; <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c> is the alt text a screen reader speaks.</p>
    <p>From <c>M13-Mehdi/source/ch-7-3.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="9-10">
<code>
&lt;figure xml:id="fig-hyp-gateway-arch"&gt;
&lt;caption&gt;
The Gateway Arch in St. Louis, Missouri. Its centerline follows the
curve &lt;m&gt;y = A\left(\cosh(Cx/L) - 1\right)&lt;/m&gt;, an upside-down catenary.
(Photograph by John Margolies, 1988; John Margolies Roadside America
photograph archive, Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs
Division.)
&lt;/caption&gt;
&lt;image source="app-catenary-gateway-arch.jpg" width="48%"&gt;
&lt;shortdescription&gt;
The stainless steel Gateway Arch rising from a line of trees against a
clear blue sky, curving up to a rounded peak and back down.
&lt;/shortdescription&gt;
&lt;/image&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Where the file lives</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Photos and alt text · step 2 of 3.</alert> Files named in <c>source</c> live in assets/. The web build copies them to external/; the PDF build reads them where they are.</p>
    <p>From <c>M13-Mehdi/publication/publication.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="2">
<code>
&lt;!-- publication/publication.ptx --&gt;
&lt;directories external="../assets" generated="../generated-assets"/&gt;

&lt;!-- so source="app-catenary-gateway-arch.jpg" is the file
     assets/app-catenary-gateway-arch.jpg, which the web build
     copies to external/ next to the pages --&gt;
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Point to it</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Photos and alt text · step 3 of 3.</alert> Any paragraph can cite the figure with <c>&lt;xref&gt;</c>. PreTeXt writes its current number and keeps it right when figures are added or moved.</p>
    <p>From <c>M13-Mehdi/source/ch-7-3.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="6">
<code>
thicker at the base than at the top. It stands 630 feet tall and 630
feet wide at the base; the National Park Service describes its
construction and its geometry at
&lt;url href="https://www.nps.gov/jeff/index.htm" visual="nps.gov"&gt;Gateway Arch National
  Park&lt;/url&gt;.
See &lt;xref ref="fig-hyp-gateway-arch"/&gt;.
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: In the web book, clicking (1.1.1) unfolds the equation right under the
       sentence (a knowl), and in-context jumps to where it lives. Every xref works this
       way with no extra markup. In a reveal.js slideshow PreTeXt prints references as
       plain text, so this slide shows the book. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Cross-references open in place</title>
    <sidebyside widths="60% 36%" valign="top">
      <image source="images/knowl.png"><shortdescription>A page of MATH 14 Section 1.1 with the reference (1.1.1) clicked open: the equation appears in a box right under the sentence, with an in-context link.</shortdescription></image>
      <stack>
        <p><alert>Click (1.1.1).</alert> The equation drops down right under the sentence. Nobody loses their place.</p>
        <p><alert>Click in-context.</alert> The page jumps to where the equation lives, inside its definition.</p>
        <p><alert>Every reference works this way:</alert> definitions, theorems, equations, figures, and exercises, with no extra markup.</p>
        <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH-14/sec-parametrization.html" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH-14">Try it in Section 1.1</url></p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- ===== How it's coded: Cross-references (3 steps) ===== -->
  <slide>
    <title>Name the target</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Cross-references · step 1 of 3.</alert> Put an <c>xml:id</c> on whatever you want to cite: here the definition and the numbered equation row inside it.</p>
    <p>From <c>MATH-14/source/section-parametrization.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="1,9">
<code>
&lt;definition xml:id="def-parametric-equations"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Parametric Equations&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;statement&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      If &lt;m&gt;x&lt;/m&gt; and &lt;m&gt;y&lt;/m&gt; are given as functions of a third
      variable &lt;m&gt;t&lt;/m&gt;, called a &lt;term&gt;parameter&lt;/term&gt;, by

    &lt;md&gt;
      &lt;mrow xml:id="eq-parametric-equations" number="yes"&gt;
        x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t),
      &lt;/mrow&gt;
    &lt;/md&gt;
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Cite it</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Cross-references · step 2 of 3.</alert> <c>&lt;xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/&gt;</c> prints the number, (1.1.1). On the web it opens the equation in place; in the PDF it is a link.</p>
    <p>From <c>MATH-14/source/section-parametrization.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="6">
<code>
&lt;p&gt;
  Here &lt;m&gt;t&lt;/m&gt; is the time parameter, which varies from some initial
  time &lt;m&gt;t_i&lt;/m&gt; to some final time &lt;m&gt;t_f&lt;/m&gt;, that is,
  &lt;m&gt;t \in [t_i, t_f]&lt;/m&gt;. Nothing has changed, except that where a
  plane curve needed the two equations
  &lt;xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/&gt;, a space curve needs three.
&lt;/p&gt;
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Cite by title</title>
    <p><alert>How it’s coded · Cross-references · step 3 of 3.</alert> <c>text="title"</c> prints the target’s title instead of its number: the assignment names the sections it draws on, and each name is a link.</p>
    <p>From <c>MATH-14/source/hw1-online.ptx</c></p>
    <program language="xml" highlight-lines="2-3">
<code>
&lt;p&gt;
  Sections: &lt;xref ref="sec-parametrization" text="title"/&gt; and
    &lt;xref ref="sec-arc-length" text="title"/&gt;. Work each problem, then type your answers
    into the boxes and have them checked here.
&lt;/p&gt;
</code>
</program>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: The book's own figure file. Choose x = c, y = c or z = c and drag c. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Interactive figures: the quadric slicer</title>
    <sidebyside widths="52% 44%" valign="top">
      <interactive xml:id="int-slide-quadric" iframe="hyperbolic-paraboloid-slices.html" aspect="8:7">
        <description><p>Slicing the hyperbolic paraboloid z = x squared over 4 minus y squared over 9 with planes x = c, y = c, and z = c.</p></description>
      </interactive>
      <stack>
        <p>Pick <m>x = c</m>, <m>y = c</m>, or <m>z = c</m> and drag <m>c</m>. The traces switch between parabolas and hyperbolas; <m>z = 0</m> gives two crossing lines.</p>
        <p>Plotly, in an ordinary web page placed with <c>&lt;interactive iframe&gt;</c>.</p>
        <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor/section-quadric-surfaces.html" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor">Open in the book</url></p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: Press Play: the plane swings from horizontal to steep, ellipse to parabola
       at m = 1 to hyperbola. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Interactive figures: cone and conics</title>
    <sidebyside widths="58% 38%" valign="top">
      <interactive xml:id="int-slide-conic" iframe="conic-slicer-manim.html" aspect="4:3">
        <description><p>A double cone cut by a tilting plane; the cut changes from ellipse to parabola to hyperbola.</p></description>
      </interactive>
      <stack>
        <p>Press Play: the plane <m>z = my + 1</m> swings from horizontal to steep. The circle stretches to an ellipse, opens into a parabola at <m>m = 1</m>, then breaks into a hyperbola.</p>
        <p>Three.js, drawn in the Manim style.</p>
        <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor/section-conics.html" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor">Open in the book</url></p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: Press Play: the tangent to the cut is green along the gradient, red against
       it, yellow along the level curve. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Interactive figures: the slice behind a directional derivative</title>
    <sidebyside widths="58% 38%" valign="top">
      <interactive xml:id="int-slide-dirderiv" iframe="dirderiv-surface-slice-manim.html" aspect="4:3">
        <description><p>The surface z = 1 + x squared + y squared cut by a vertical plane through P zero that turns around; the tangent to the cut is colored by direction.</p></description>
      </interactive>
      <stack>
        <p>Press Play: the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1, 1)</m> turns once around. The tangent to the cut is steepest along <m>\nabla f</m>, steepest down along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and level along the level curve.</p>
        <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor/section-directional-derivatives.html" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH13-Instructor">Open in the book</url></p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: Circle the low, then Run for open water, and watch the barometer. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Interactive figures: sailing the isobars of a low</title>
    <sidebyside widths="62% 34%" valign="top">
      <interactive xml:id="int-slide-isobars" iframe="isobars-low-3d.html" aspect="16:10">
        <description><p>The pressure field around a low, with a ship that either circles the eye along an isobar or runs outward along the gradient, and a barometer reading.</p></description>
      </interactive>
      <stack>
        <p>Press <q>Circle the low</q>, then <q>Run for open water</q>, and watch the barometer: along an isobar the pressure stays put; across them it changes fastest.</p>
        <p>Web only: the PDF prints a sentence in its place.</p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: MATH 14. This one is not an iframe page but a script with a slate, the
       second way of embedding. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Interactive figures: a script and a slate (MATH 14)</title>
    <sidebyside widths="44% 52%" valign="top">
      <interactive xml:id="int-slide-polar-rose" platform="javascript" source="polar-widget.js" aspect="10:11">
        <slate xml:id="int-slide-polar-rose-slate" surface="html">
<div data-widget="rose" style="text-align:center; font-family: 'IBM Plex Sans', 'Open Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;">
<canvas width="460" height="460" style="width:460px; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;"></canvas>
<div style="margin-top:0.6em; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; gap:0.7em; flex-wrap:wrap;">
<label for="rose-theta" style="font-family: 'Source Serif 4', 'PT Serif', Times, serif; font-style:italic; font-size:1.2em;">&#952;</label>
<input id="rose-theta" type="range" min="0" max="48" value="0" step="1" style="width:60%; max-width:340px;"/>
</div>
<div class="pw-readout" style="margin-top:0.5em; line-height:1.5;"></div>
</div>
        </slate>
      </interactive>
      <stack>
        <p>The rose <m>r = \cos 3\theta</m> and the circle <m>r = \tfrac12</m>. Drag <m>\theta</m>: the dots mark where the ray meets each curve, which is what fixes the limits of <m>r</m> in the double integral.</p>
        <p><c>platform="javascript"</c> loads a script; <c>&lt;slate surface="html"&gt;</c> holds the canvas and slider it draws into.</p>
        <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH-14-Instructor/section-double-integrals-polar-form.html" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH-14-Instructor">Open in the book</url></p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- ===== How it's coded: The quadric slicer (5 steps) ===== -->
  <slide>
    <title>An ordinary web page</title>
